<speak>
<p>Let u be any positive integer. We combine the exact sign formula with Gaussian parity. E of P equals minus H three of P, plus the product of the quantity i minus one with the half-sum of H one and H two. The half-sum has integer coefficients.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>Since i minus one equals i multiplied by the quantity one plus i, the whole second term is divisible by one plus i. Under coefficient reduction it vanishes, leaving E of P congruent to minus H three of P. The exact integer half-sum was formed before this reduction; we are not dividing in the residue ring.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>Write H three as the sum of h sub three r times P to the r, for r from zero to u squared minus one. Each sign is plus or minus one. Both possible values of its negative have residue one. Thus E of P is congruent to J of P, the sum of P to the r over those same positions. Every coefficient of E minus J is divisible by one plus i. This uses the common signs, without norm identities or oddness of u.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>For u equal to three, there are nine coefficient positions. Here is an illustrative row of allowed signs for minus H three. Keep all nine powers, from P to the zero through P to the eighth, fixed. Reduce each coefficient to one. These are residues: the original coefficients of E need not equal one.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>Now substitute P equal to one. Every power in J becomes one. Therefore E of one is congruent to J of one, and J of one equals u squared. This evaluation statement is different from the polynomial congruence we have already proved.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>Only now assume that u is odd. Then u squared is odd. In the nine-term example, four pairs of ones vanish because two has residue zero, and one remains. In general write u squared as two k plus one. The k pairs vanish in characteristic two, leaving one. Hence E of one is congruent to one modulo one plus i.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>Carry forward the coefficient congruence, together with the exact sign divisibilities from the earlier node: two divides d of P minus c of P, and one plus i divides g of P minus c of P, coefficient by coefficient. Those differences supply the later sign comparisons. The all-ones congruence will enter the final contradiction after the product identities become an additive relation.<break time="1000ms"/></p>
</speak>
